**Proposition 22.5 (strict extension, with serial collision preserved).**
The block domain strictly extends the single-birth separated domain
after identifying a singleton block with its member. The run

$$
P_\varepsilon K S_\varepsilon K
 (S_LP_R)
\tag{22.3}
$$

and the run with last block $(P_RS_L)$ are admitted, have identical
block Time and endpoint, and have different chosen serial orders.
They are outside Definition 3.2's single-birth separation.
The unseparated counterpair in Boundary 4.4 remains a collision.

Proof. After $P_\varepsilon$ and $S_\varepsilon$ the logical children
are beta at $L$ and alpha at $R$. Thus $S_L$ and $P_R$ are enabled
at distinct pre-block leaves, and commute by Theorem 21.2.
The preceding two gaps are positive; the final gap is allowed zero.
There is no Tick between those last two births, violating the old
single-birth domain. Every singleton-block history has the old
recurrence, so its inverse agrees by Theorem 4.1.
Boundary 4.4's all-unticked pair performs dependent edits in the
original root cone and is not a single independent block. Its two
raw zero-extended fields remain one at root and zero elsewhere;
none of the present arguments changes that computation. $\square$

